<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:theme="using:AngelSix.ThemeEngine.Generated">
  <!--
    A consumer's own style classes, living downstream of the theme library.

    This is the other half of extending a theme. BrandTheme adds an accent *hue*, which needs
    nothing beyond declaring the colours — theme:Accent.Kind="Brand" then works. This is the other
    thing you are likely to want: variants of your own on top of the theme's controls.

    Written as a Styles collection layering setters over the theme's ControlThemes, rather than as a
    ControlTheme of its own. That is the right shape for "the theme's button, but ours too":

      - The theme's Button ControlTheme still supplies the template and every state, so a brand
        button stays a normal button and keeps working when the theme changes.
      - A ControlTheme would mean BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}", and the theme's
        control dictionaries are x:ClassModifier="internal" — deliberately not consumer API — so
        that resource cannot be reached from here at all.
      - Top-level selectors like Button.hero are visible to static analysis as they stand, so these
        classes need no StyleClasses.axaml declarations. That file is for classes declared as
        ^.name *inside* a ControlTheme, which is the theme library's case, not this one.

    Values come from theme tokens rather than literals, so a brand button tracks the theme's spacing
    and type scale instead of drifting away from everything around it. They come from
    AngelSix.ThemeEngine.Generated — the shared namespace holding every {theme:Token}, the
    hand-written extensions like Edges, and AccentBrush. This project's own generated namespace
    (…Generated.AvaloniaThemeLab, used in MainWindow) holds only its accent types.
  -->

  <!-- Filled brand button. AccentBrush resolves against the hue the control carries, so this one
       style serves Brand, Success or anything else the theme declares. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.hero">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{theme:AccentBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{theme:SurfaceBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{theme:FontWeightBold}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{theme:Edges Horizontal={theme:SpacingXxl}, Vertical={theme:SpacingLg}}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- These states have to be restated: an app-level Style outranks the ControlTheme's own
       :pointerover, so without them a hero button would keep its rest background on hover. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.hero:pointerover">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{theme:AccentBrush Dark1}" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="Button.hero:pressed">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{theme:AccentBrush Dark2}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Text-only counterpart, for the secondary action beside a hero. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.quiet">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{theme:OnSurfaceDimBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="Button.quiet:pointerover">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{theme:AccentBrush}" />
  </Style>

</Styles>
